```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "v08-working-with-chains-",
                      fig.width = 7, fig.height = 5, dpi = 96)
library(bayespulse)
library(ggplot2)
set.seed(2026)
```

Every plot in this vignette series -- `bp_trace()`, `bp_posteriors()`,
`bp_location_posterior()`, `bp_predicted()` -- is a convenience wrapper
around the same underlying objects: tidy tables of posterior draws stored in
the fit. When the packaged plots stop matching the question you are asking,
go to the draws directly. This vignette shows how: extracting the chains,
building custom summaries and derived quantities, and running
posterior-predictive checks.

## 1. A fit to work with

The same simulated series, specification, and fit as the getting-started
vignette. (Precomputed offline via `vignettes/precompute.R`; about a minute
at this chain length.)

```{r fit, results = "hide"}
simdat <- simulate_pulse(num_obs = 60, interval = 10, error_var = 0.005,
                         ipi_mean = 12, ipi_var = 10, ipi_min = 4,
                         pulse_distribution = "lognormal",
                         mass_mean = 0.9, mass_sd = 0.3,
                         width_mean = 4.6, width_sd = 0.3,
                         constant_halflife = 40, constant_baseline = 2.5)

spec <- pulse_spec(
  location_prior_type    = "strauss",
  prior_location_gamma   = 0.1,  prior_location_range = 30,
  prior_halflife_mean    = 38,   prior_halflife_var   = 1000,
  prior_baseline_mean    = 2.25, prior_baseline_var   = 100,
  prior_mean_pulse_count = 10,
  sv_baseline_mean = 2.5, sv_halflife_mean = 42, sv_error_var = 0.005,
  pv_pulse_location = 5)

fit <- fit_pulse(data = simdat$data, spec = spec,
                 iters = 250000, thin = 100, burnin = 25000, verbose = FALSE)
```

## 2. The common-parameter chain

`patient_chain()` returns one row per saved draw of the subject-level
parameters, keyed by `iteration`:

```{r patient-chain}
chain <- patient_chain(fit)
head(chain, 3)
```

With a plain data frame in hand, any summary is a line of base R. A
quantile table for every parameter:

```{r summaries}
params <- setdiff(names(chain), "iteration")
t(sapply(chain[params], quantile, probs = c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975)))
```

Derived quantities are just transformations of the draws -- transform first,
summarize after, and the uncertainty propagates itself. Two examples that
answer natural-scale questions about log-scale parameters:

```{r derived}
# Posterior for the *median pulse mass* on the natural concentration scale
# (mass_mean is the mean of log mass, so exp(mass_mean) is the median mass)
quantile(exp(chain$mass_mean), probs = c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975))

# Posterior probability that the half-life exceeds 45 minutes
mean(chain$halflife > 45)
```

The first line is worth pausing on: a 95% interval for the natural-scale
median pulse mass is awkward to read off the log-scale posterior summaries,
but is one `exp()` away when you work with draws.

## 3. The per-pulse chain

`pulse_chain()` returns the pulse-level draws in long format: one row per
pulse per saved draw, again keyed by `iteration`. Because the number of
pulses is itself sampled, different draws contribute different numbers of
rows -- exactly the kind of ragged structure a long table handles naturally.

```{r pulse-chain}
pulses <- pulse_chain(fit)
head(pulses, 3)
nrow(pulses) / nrow(chain)  # average pulses per draw
```

(`eta_mass` and `eta_width` are the per-pulse Student-t scale factors; under
the default Gaussian pulse random effects they are fixed at 1.)

A hand-rolled version of `bp_location_posterior()` -- with the true simulated
pulse locations overlaid, which the packaged plot cannot know about:

```{r locations, fig.height = 3.5}
ggplot(pulses, aes(location)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 10, boundary = 0,
                 fill = "grey70", color = "grey40", linewidth = 0.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = simdat$parameters$location,
             linetype = "dashed", color = "firebrick") +
  labs(x = "Pulse location (minutes)",
       y = "Draws containing a pulse in bin") +
  theme_minimal()
```

Each dashed line is a true pulse location; the histogram is where the
posterior places pulses across all saved draws. This is the same information
`bp_location_posterior(fit)` shows -- the point is that owning the draws
lets you add whatever your question needs, here the ground truth.

## 4. The fitted curve, by hand

`predict()` returns a tidy table (time, posterior mean and median
concentration, credible bounds), so the packaged `bp_predicted()` plot is
easy to rebuild -- and restyle -- yourself:

```{r predicted, fig.height = 3.5}
pred <- predict(fit, cred_interval = 0.9)
ggplot(pred, aes(time)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), fill = "steelblue",
              alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(aes(y = mean.conc), color = "steelblue") +
  geom_point(data = simdat$data, aes(time, concentration), size = 0.8) +
  labs(x = "Time (minutes)", y = "Concentration") +
  theme_minimal()
```

This answers "does the fitted curve pass through the data?" -- a check of
fit at the level of the mean. The next section asks a stronger question.

## 5. Posterior-predictive checks

A posterior-predictive check asks: *if the fitted model were true, would
data like mine be typical?* For each of a sample of posterior draws, we
push the drawn subject-level parameters back through the package's own
simulator, generating a full replicate dataset -- new pulse train, new
pulses, new noise -- and compare summary statistics of the replicates
against the observed series.

One honest limitation to state up front: the pulse-timing regime
(`ipi_mean`, `ipi_var`) is a *design input* to the simulator, not a
parameter the model estimates, so we hold it at its design value. The check
below therefore probes the concentration-scale behaviour (level, spread,
peaks), not the timing model.

```{r ppc, results = "hide"}
stat_fun <- function(y) c(mean = mean(y), sd = sd(y), max = max(y))

set.seed(11)
draw_ids <- sample(chain$iteration, 200)
ppc <- do.call(rbind, lapply(draw_ids, function(g) {
  d <- chain[chain$iteration == g, ]
  rep_sim <- simulate_pulse(num_obs = 60, interval = 10,
                            error_var = d$model_error,
                            ipi_mean = 12, ipi_var = 10, ipi_min = 4,
                            pulse_distribution = "lognormal",
                            mass_mean = d$mass_mean, mass_sd = d$mass_sd,
                            width_mean = d$width_mean, width_sd = d$width_sd,
                            constant_halflife = d$halflife,
                            constant_baseline = d$baseline)
  stat_fun(rep_sim$data$concentration)
}))
observed <- stat_fun(simdat$data$concentration)
```

Before summarizing, check the replicates themselves:

```{r ppc-clean}
finite_rep <- apply(is.finite(ppc), 1, all)
sum(!finite_rep)
ppc <- ppc[finite_rep, ]
```

A few replicates come back non-finite, and the reason is worth knowing: each
one carries a `width_sd` draw from near the top of its Uniform(0, 10) prior.
The convergence and simulation-study vignettes both flagged `width_sd` as
weakly identified -- its posterior stays close to its prior -- and this is
what that abstraction means in data space. A log-scale pulse-width SD of 9
implies individual pulse widths spanning many orders of magnitude, which no
finite hormone series exhibits and which overflow the simulator. The
posterior-predictive loop surfaces those draws honestly; we drop them and
note the count rather than pretend they were never sampled.

```{r ppc-plot, fig.height = 3.5}
ppc_long <- data.frame(statistic = rep(colnames(ppc), each = nrow(ppc)),
                       value = as.vector(ppc))
obs_long <- data.frame(statistic = names(observed), value = observed)
ggplot(ppc_long, aes(value)) +
  geom_density(fill = "grey80", color = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(data = obs_long, aes(xintercept = value),
             color = "firebrick", linewidth = 0.7) +
  facet_wrap(~ statistic, scales = "free") +
  labs(x = "Statistic value across replicate datasets",
       y = "Density") +
  theme_minimal()
```

```{r ppc-pvals}
round(colMeans(sweep(ppc, 2, observed, ">=")), 2)
```

The red line is the observed value of each statistic; the grey density is
its distribution across replicate datasets generated from the posterior.
Values near 0 or 1 in the tail-probability table would flag a statistic the
model systematically fails to reproduce; mid-range values mean the observed
data look like a typical draw from the fitted model. Checks like this are
cheap to extend -- swap in any statistic your application cares about
(trough level, peak-to-trough ratio, time above a threshold) by changing
`stat_fun()`.

## 6. Where this leaves the series

This closes the vignette ladder: simulating and understanding the model
(anatomy, priors), fitting and checking a single subject (getting started,
convergence), pooling subjects (population), coupling hormones (joint), and
quantifying trust and design (simulation study). The chains extracted here
are the common currency underneath all of it -- when a packaged summary
does not answer your question, the draws will.
